42.m:
Split in two like a piece of wood, he fell to the surface of the earth.
Even so, the mighty Rāhu, lord of the Dānavas, did not release [him];
he made an effort to free himself, but could not, O Nārada.

43.m:
the unmanifest and the manifest, those with qualities and those devoid of qualities.
Having seen them, the Bhārgava, filled with wonder, wandered around;
while the Bhārgava remained there, a divine year passed.

The greatly valorous Vināyaka, wielding an axe in battle,
enraged, fought Tuhuṇḍa, the best of the rākṣasas;
and Duryodhana fought the mighty Ghaṇṭākarṇa.

45.m:
Thus long ago, in terror, he cleaved with his thunderbolt even his own brothers while still in the womb to save himself;
therefore, O great sage, the lord Mahendra became celebrated as Gotrabhit.

Thus ends the forty-fifth chapter in the glorious Vāmana Purāṇa.

49.m:
sacred tradition, sacred law, constancy, renown, beauty, and peace endowed with action;
nourishment, contentment, and also beauty, as well as the virtues residing in purity—
all of them, resorting to Bali, rested at ease.

While the lord of Dānavas ruled heaven, no one was afflicted by hunger, soiled, or distressed;
even an ordinary man became ever-radiant, devoted to righteousness, self-restrained, and an enjoyer of pleasures.

Thus ends the forty-ninth chapter in the glorious Vāmana Purāṇa.

50.m:
and where formerly the difficult sacrifice celebrated among gods and asuras was known as the Mahāmedha;
where the blessed Murāri, possessing an unmanifest body and having the form of space, made his residence,
and then attained renown as Gadādhara, a sharp axe to the tree of great sin;

where the foremost Brahmins, even if destitute of the Vedas and treatises, attain equality with the Grandfather;
and where, having worshipped the ancestors once with devotion and with single-minded consciousness,
men obtain the infinite fruit of the Mahāmedha sacrifice by the grace of the Blessed One;
